@prefix ag:   <http://example.org/agora#> .
@prefix sosa: <http://www.w3.org/ns/sosa/> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<http://example.org/agora/simulated-sensing> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Agora simulated sensing" ;
    rdfs:comment """Perceiving a subject that has no sensor, by holding a MODEL of it.

This is a separate capability with a separate vocabulary, not a mode of the real one. Nothing here appears in a production world and nothing in a production world appears here — an agent is wired to a real sensor or to a modelled one, never to a thing that could be either depending on a flag. A world that simulates is a different world, and you can tell by reading it.

What it deliberately shares is the FAMILY: ag:SimulatedSensing is an ag:PerceptionCapability, so an agent that needs "whoever perceives" finds it without knowing which kind it got. That is the point of families — see capabilities/perception/ontology.ttl.""" .

#################  The capability  #################

ag:SimulatedSensing a ag:PerceptionCapability ; rdfs:label "Simulated sensing" ;
    rdfs:comment "The agent holds a model of its subject and reads that. It owns the clock completely, having no device to negotiate with — which makes it the only member of the perception family with no counterpart on the wire." .

#################  What it is wired to  #################

ag:ModelledSubject a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf ag:Sensor ; rdfs:label "Modelled subject" ;
    rdfs:comment "A sensor with no device behind it: the value is computed rather than measured. It IS a sensor — it monitors one subject, observes one property, and must say so — because everything downstream is identical and only the origin differs. Declaring the relationship here rather than in the perception package is the rule: a capability may reference another's TERMS, never its code." .

ag:Modelled a ag:SenseMode ; rdfs:label "Modelled" ;
    rdfs:comment "There is no device to drive, so there is nothing to ask and nothing to instruct. A fourth value on perception's own axis, which is what keeps the modes mutually exclusive: a sensor has exactly one, so a modelled one can never also derive a capability meant for hardware." .

ag:models a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:subPropertyOf ag:polls ;
    rdfs:domain ag:Agent ; rdfs:range ag:ModelledSubject ;
    rdfs:label "models" ;
    rdfs:comment "The agent computes this subject's state. A sub-property of ag:polls because it IS the same access grant — this agent, and no other, may read this thing — so every rule written about perceiving applies unchanged. It is a distinct property so that wiring meant for hardware can never accidentally grant a simulation, or the reverse." .

#################  The physics — facts about the world, not opinions  #################
# A real subject dries too. The difference is only that here somebody has to compute it, so
# the world must state the rate it does so at.

# Named apart from the domain's own physics on purpose. ag:dryRatePerTick already exists in
# domain/water with rdfs:domain ag:Plant, so reusing it would make every model infer as a
# plant and inherit a plant's obligations. Separate ontologies mean separate terms.

ag:modelledDryRate a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:ModelledSubject ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "modelled dry rate" ;
    rdfs:comment "How much of the observed property the model loses each tick." .

ag:modelledTickSeconds a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:ModelledSubject ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "modelled tick seconds" ;
    rdfs:comment "Wall-clock seconds per tick — how fast this simulated world runs. On the DEVICE rather than in anyone's beliefs, because it is a property of the model, not an opinion an agent could hold about it." .

ag:modelledInitialValue a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain ag:ModelledSubject ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "modelled initial value" ;
    rdfs:comment "What the model reads before anything has happened to it." .
